		{
			"id": "109445845587715421",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@ericcarlisle/109445845560998562",
			"author": {
				"name": "Eric Carlisle",
				"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@ericcarlisle",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/310/162/045/975/533/original/d6baf7dd32f355b6.png"
			},
			"date": "2022-12-02T19:57:51.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> : Thinking on that. Would it be fair to rephrase that question to \"Should we create websites to be PWAs?\" 🤔 <br>That's a pretty easy ask when the website is a self-reliant tool (a to-do app, converter, etc...) that client-side code can do on its own.<br>I think things get more complicated when we have sites w/ live data - e.g. news sites, streaming services, communication. Such services could have an offline mode, but I'm wondering the value that would offer.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
